---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { getSite } from '../lib/sanity';
const site = await getSite();
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="What kyudo is, the dojo's name 練誠館 (Hall of Sincere Training), its history since the late 1970s, and its place in the Northern California Kyudo Federation and the American Kyudo Renmei."
>
  <section id="about" class="page" aria-labelledby="about-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="about-mon" id="aboutMon" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <PageHeader eyebrow="About the dojo" title="練誠館, the Renseikan" id="about-title">
        <p class="lede">
          Kyudo (弓道, "the way of the bow") is Japanese archery practised as a discipline: one arrow at a time, the
          same eight movements, the same breath. {site.name} teaches it every Sunday at the Japanese Art &amp; Cultural
          Center (JACC) in San Jose, California.
        </p>
      </PageHeader>
      <h3>The name</h3>
      <p>
        練 <em>ren</em> is to train, to refine; 誠 <em>sei</em> is sincerity; 館 <em>kan</em> is the hall. Together, the
        Hall of Sincere Training.
      </p>
      <h3>History</h3>
      <p>
        The Renseikan began as the San Jose Kyudo Club, formed in the late 1970s. The club was the original founding
        dojo of the{' '}
        <a href="https://www.nckf.org" rel="noopener">
          Northern California Kyudo Federation
        </a>{' '}
        (NCKF), which Japan accepted as a foreign <em>renmei</em> in 1981.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sensei Steve Scott renamed the San Jose Kyudo Club the Renseikan in 2003, a few years after the Kurumi-an dojo
        in San Jose closed in 1999.
      </p>
      <h3>What a class looks like</h3>
      <p>
        The dojo usually sets up three makiwara (straw practice targets shot at close range) and students take turns at
        them and at the mato 28 metres away. Beginners learn the eight stages of the shot (the <em>shahō hassetsu</em>)
        with a rubber practice bow called a gomuyumi before moving to a bow the dojo lends. Next come the glove and the
        first arrows at the makiwara. The mato comes once the form is steady. Nothing is rushed and a class has room for
        everyone to shoot.
      </p>
      <h3>Affiliation</h3>
      <p>
        Renseikan is the founding dojo of the{' '}
        <a href="https://www.nckf.org" rel="noopener">
          Northern California Kyudo Federation
        </a>
        (NCKF) and a member of the{' '}
        <a href="https://www.kyudo.us" rel="noopener">
          American Kyudo Renmei
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
